          "id": "sec-2-13",
          "secNumber": "2.13",
          "heading": "Experimental Laboratory Investigation: Laboratory 18 \u2014 Heat recovery minimum approach",
          "content": "# Laboratory 18 \u2014 Heat recovery minimum approach\n\n**Linked unit:** 8. **Measured output:** Optimistic recovery ceiling (kW). **Equation:** `Qscreen=Cmin max(0,Thi\u2212Tci\u2212\u0394Tmin)`.\n\n## Experiment objective and theory\n\nThis interactive experiment isolates one thermal relationship so that parameter sensitivity can be observed without the distraction of a full process model. It does not solve an entire plant; the objective is to learn which inputs the mathematical expression regards as controlling, which inputs the engineer would have to obtain from measurements, and which unmodeled effects could reverse an apparent benefit. Start by writing the direction of expected heat transfer. For an entirely passive system, check that the response is compatible with the stated hot and cold temperatures and the sign convention.\n\n**Validity boundary:** Screening ceiling only; not a network pinch analysis or exchanger rating. The simplified correlation treats the remaining conditions as fixed even if that assumption would not survive an actual change in flow, phase or material temperature. If the laboratory displays a finite number at an invalid combination, do not call that number a validated prediction. Temperature-dependent properties should be evaluated at a defensible reference state; this model deliberately does not call a property database.\n\n## Default test and sensitivity measurements\n\nPrimary variable: **minimum approach**, unit **K**. Initial value: **12 K**. Predicted output: **313.6 kW**. Your result may round on the web page. Confirm the orders of magnitude independently with the printed equation.\n\nAt the first selected test value **11.6 K**, the reference model gives **314.88 kW**. At the second test value **40.4 K**, it gives **222.72 kW**. Do not draw a straight-line conclusion from these two numbers without checking the full response curve. Export the CSV, calculate a central finite difference near the default value, and compare it with the curvature of the graph. Keep the parameter-change interval small enough for a sensitivity calculation but large enough to avoid rounding effects.\n\n| Control | Default | Units |\n|---|---:|---|\n| minimum approach | 12 | K |\n| Ch | 4600 | W/K |\n| Cc | 3200 | W/K |\n| Th | 145 | \u00b0C |\n| Tc | 35 | \u00b0C |\n\n## Controlled-variable experiment\n\n1. Record the baseline output and plot before touching any slider. Explain whether a temperature difference, resistance, heat capacity or dimensionless correlation establishes the result.\n2. Move the primary slider to its low and high limits. Identify the graph region where an extrapolation of the physics would be most dangerous.\n3. Restore the baseline. Change one secondary material or geometry parameter by approximately 10%. State the expected direction before observing it; then describe the difference between your prediction and the numerical response.\n4. Use reset to reproduce the default experiment, and export the CSV. Check the exported data for units, sign and monotonicity. A curve with a sharp numerical discontinuity should be investigated rather than smoothed away automatically.\n5. Prepare a four-column comparison of baseline, adjusted input, adjusted output and physical interpretation. Finish with a statement of the lab model's limitations and what one real measurement would validate it.\n\n## Interpretation and field extension\n\nA computational curve is a relationship under controlled assumptions, not a set of experimental observations. In a plant, thermal conductivity, viscosity and heat capacity change with temperature; fouling deposits grow in time; and equipment area can be effectively reduced by flow maldistribution. Explain whether the chosen experiment can distinguish these effects. If not, propose a paired measurement, such as wall temperatures and stream flow, that creates additional evidence. Never claim improved process safety or energy economics solely from a single slider result.\n\nQuestions: (a) Which input appears with the highest exponent in the local law? (b) Could an unmeasured contact resistance mimic the same trend? (c) Does the graph represent a local coefficient, an area-average value or a whole-unit thermal duty? (d) What would an alternative geometry change? (e) What independent instrument or property reference would be required before industrial scale-up?"
